1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,309
In 1924, the world's biggest lightbulb makers

2
00:00:03,309 --> 00:00:04,979
made a secret deal.

3
00:00:04,979 --> 00:00:07,243
Make the bulb... worse.

4
00:00:07,243 --> 00:00:09,170
Companies from Germany, the Netherlands,

5
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:11,246
France, Britain and Japan,

6
00:00:11,246 --> 00:00:12,708
plus an American giant

7
00:00:12,708 --> 00:00:14,584
working through its overseas arms.

8
00:00:14,584 --> 00:00:16,117
Back then, a good bulb

9
00:00:16,117 --> 00:00:17,883
could burn up to 2,000 hours.

10
00:00:17,883 --> 00:00:19,056
The new rule:

11
00:00:19,056 --> 00:00:21,135
1,000. Max.

12
00:00:21,135 --> 00:00:22,935
Every factory mailed sample bulbs

13
00:00:22,935 --> 00:00:24,300
to a lab in Switzerland.

14
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,297
Bulb lasted too long?

15
00:00:26,297 --> 00:00:27,779
The factory paid a fine.

16
00:00:27,779 --> 00:00:29,084
And it worked.

17
00:00:29,084 --> 00:00:31,622
Real bulb life dropped by about a third.

18
00:00:31,622 --> 00:00:32,980
Here's the twist:

19
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:34,606
bulbs got cheaper to make,

20
00:00:34,606 --> 00:00:36,226
but prices barely moved.

21
00:00:36,226 --> 00:00:37,747
Because every bulb that burns out

22
00:00:37,747 --> 00:00:39,282
is a bulb you buy again.

23
00:00:39,282 --> 00:00:41,929
The deal only died in 1940,

24
00:00:41,929 --> 00:00:43,682
when war tore it apart.

25
00:00:43,682 --> 00:00:45,772
Your bulb didn't just burn out.

26
00:00:45,772 --> 00:00:47,010
It was scheduled to.
